# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
QUIZ = {"07": [
    ("A patient arrests on the machine. What is the circuit nurse&rsquo;s first action?",
     ["Stop fluid removal", "Start compressions", "Return the blood without checking", "Remove the needles"], 0,
     "Best: fluid removal empties a circulation that has none to spare; stop it at once. Why not the others: another person compresses; the blood decision comes after checking for air, hemolysis or reaction; needles stay in for drugs."),
    ("Foam is seen below the venous chamber in a patient who collapses. What is the blood decision?",
     ["Stop the pump, clamp the venous line, and do not return the blood", "Return the blood quickly to restore volume", "Override the air detector to finish rinse-back", "Disconnect the arterial line only"], 0,
     "Best: returning the blood pushes more air into the patient (Card 5). Why not the others: rinse-back and overriding the detector add air; partial disconnection does not stop the air."),
    ("The venous line blood looks translucent and port-wine colored during an arrest. What do you do with the circuit blood?",
     ["Clamp and do not return it", "Return it to treat hypovolemia", "Return half of it", "Filter it through a new dialyzer first"], 0,
     "Best: hemolyzed blood carries potassium from broken red cells and is never returned. Why not the others: any return adds potassium; filtering is not a bedside option."),
    ("With no air, hemolysis, reaction or open line, what are the two acceptable blood options?",
     ["Return the blood quickly with saline, or clamp and disconnect", "Leave the pump running at full speed", "Keep fluid removal going to protect the lungs", "Wait for the physician before touching the machine"], 0,
     "Best: these match the ERC principle and Card 1. Why not the others: running the circuit or fluid removal in arrest is unsafe; waiting leaves the circuit unmanaged."),
    ("What does ERC 2025 advise about defibrillation with a patient connected to the dialysis machine?",
     ["Disconnect unless the equipment is defibrillation-proof, and beware of wet surfaces", "Always defibrillate while connected", "Never defibrillate a dialysis patient", "Turn off the machine power only"], 0,
     "Best: the ERC recommends disconnection unless defibrillation-proof and warns about wet surfaces. Why not the others: connection without a rating is a hazard; dialysis patients are defibrillated; power-off alone does not address the patient connection."),
    ("Where do you find out whether your dialysis machine is defibrillation-proof?",
     ["The manufacturer&rsquo;s instructions for use for that model", "A general resuscitation guideline", "The patient&rsquo;s chart", "The crash cart checklist from another unit"], 0,
     "Best: the rating belongs to the device and model. Why not the others: guidelines, charts and other units&rsquo; lists do not state your device&rsquo;s rating."),
    ("Before giving epinephrine through a dialysis catheter limb, what must be done?",
     ["Aspirate the lock solution (at least the labeled limb volume), then flush with saline", "Push the drug directly; the lock does not matter", "Inject air to clear the limb", "Add the drug to the lock solution"], 0,
     "Best: the lock may be concentrated heparin or citrate; aspirating prevents giving it to the patient. Why not the others: pushing the lock is a medication error; air is dangerous; mixing with the lock is wrong."),
    ("What does &lsquo;leave the access open&rsquo; mean in the ERC guidance?",
     ["Keep the access available and working for drugs, clamped and secured between uses", "Leave the catheter limbs uncapped", "Remove the clamps so drugs flow freely", "Leave the needles untaped"], 0,
     "Best: open means available, never an uncapped needle or unclamped limb, which can draw in air or bleed. Why not the others: each describes an unsafe open access."),
    ("Which position does the 2025 AHA guideline recommend for life-threatening gas embolism?",
     ["Supine or recovery position on a level surface", "Sitting upright", "Prone", "Head up at 45 degrees"], 0,
     "Best: AHA 2025 (COR 2a, LOE C-EO); dialysis teaching of the left side differs, and Card 5 defers to protocol. Why not the others: sitting or head-up can send air to the brain; prone is not recommended."),
    ("Why must the circuit nurse let go of the blood lines when the leader calls &lsquo;clear&rsquo;?",
     ["The lines are in contact with the patient and can carry current during a shock", "To prevent the needles from falling out", "To let the machine alarm", "Because the pump must restart"], 0,
     "Best: anything touching the patient during the shock can carry current to staff. Why not the others: they are not the reason for clearing."),
]}
